course: religion-ethics
lesson: 10
title: Daniel, Jonah and the prophets
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Prophets were messengers who spoke God's word: they warned kings and people about injustice and gave hope in hard times.
explain: Daniel and his friends, captives in Babylon, kept God's food laws; Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego would not bow to a golden statue and walked unharmed in the fiery furnace; Daniel kept praying despite a law against it and was kept safe in the lions' den, and he read the writing on the wall that warned a proud king his kingdom would end.
explain: Jonah was sent to warn Nineveh but ran away on a ship; thrown into the sea, he was swallowed by a great fish, and when he finally preached, the whole city said sorry and God spared it, which made Jonah angry; God then taught him with a plant to care for a city of people more than for a plant.
explain: The prophet Isaiah dreamed of a world where the wolf lives with the lamb and swords are beaten into ploughshares.
explain: For a child today these stories mean: you can be respectful where you live and still stay true to your faith, God gives second chances even to people we dislike, and speaking up for those treated unfairly is a calling.
fact: Prophets were messengers who spoke God's word, warned against injustice and gave hope.
fact: Daniel and his friends, captives in Babylon, kept God's food laws and stayed healthy on vegetables and water.
fact: Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego refused to bow to a golden statue and said they would stay faithful even if God did not rescue them.
fact: The three friends were thrown into a fiery furnace but were not burnt.
fact: Daniel kept praying to God three times a day despite a law forbidding it, was thrown to the lions and was kept safe.
fact: At King Belshazzar's feast a hand wrote on the palace wall, and Daniel explained that the king's kingdom would end; «the writing on the wall» now means a clear warning.
fact: God sent Jonah to warn the city of Nineveh, but Jonah ran away on a ship going the other way.
fact: In a storm Jonah was thrown overboard, swallowed by a great fish and spat out on land after three days.
fact: Jonah preached, the whole city of Nineveh repented, and God spared it; Jonah was angry about this.
fact: God gave Jonah a plant for shade and let it die, to teach him to care for a city of people more than for a plant; the book ends with God's question to Jonah, left for every reader to answer.
fact: Christians differ on whether to read Jonah as history or as a teaching story; this is disputed.
fact: Isaiah foresaw a peaceable kingdom where the wolf lives with the lamb and swords are beaten into ploughshares, the blades of ploughs: weapons turned into tools for farming.
exercise: What was a prophet's job? | To speak God's word, warn against injustice and give hope | To rule the country as king | To guard the Temple gates | To collect taxes for the king
exercise: Why did Daniel refuse the king's food? | He wanted to keep God's food laws | The food tasted far too spicy for him | He was not at all hungry in Babylon | The king's cook was his secret enemy
exercise: What happened to Daniel and his friends on their simple food? | They stayed healthy and strong | They grew weak, thin and very ill | They were all sent back home to Judah | They were made to eat the king's meat
exercise: What did Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego refuse to do? | Bow down to a golden statue | Pay taxes to the king | Learn the language of Babylon | Work in the king's palace
exercise: What did the three friends say before the furnace? | They would stay faithful even if God did not rescue them | They would bow down if the fire was made hot enough to scare them | They would fight the king's soldiers | They would run away to Jerusalem
exercise: What happened in the fiery furnace? | The three friends were not burnt | They escaped through a tunnel | The fire went out by itself | The king pulled them out at once
exercise: Why was Daniel thrown to the lions? | He kept praying to God despite a law forbidding it | He stole gold and silver from the king's treasury in the night | He refused to eat the king's food | He insulted the king at a feast
exercise: What happened to Daniel in the lions' den? | He was kept safe all night | He fought the lions and won | He tamed the lions with music | He escaped before morning
exercise: What does «the writing on the wall» mean today? | A clear warning that something will end | A friendly message from a friend at school | Graffiti sprayed on a wall of the school building | A list of the rules for a big royal feast
exercise: Where did God send Jonah? | To the city of Nineveh | To the city of Babylon | To the land of Egypt | To Mount Sinai
exercise: What did Jonah do instead of obeying? | He ran away on a ship going the other way | He hid in the Temple | He stayed at home and slept | He went to Babylon to see Daniel and ask for his advice
exercise: What happened to Jonah in the storm? | He was thrown overboard and swallowed by a great fish | He swam to the nearest island | He steered the ship safely to land through the wind and the waves | He was rescued by a fishing boat
exercise: How long was Jonah inside the great fish? | Three days | Forty days | Seven days | One hour
exercise: What did Nineveh do when Jonah preached? | The whole city repented and God spared it | The whole city laughed at him and was destroyed by fire | The king threw Jonah to the lions | The people built a tower
exercise: How did Jonah feel when God forgave Nineveh? | Angry, because he wanted his enemies punished | Happy and proud that his preaching had worked so well | Afraid that the great fish would come back for him | Sorry that he had ever run away from God at all
exercise: What did God teach Jonah with the plant? | To care for a city of people more than a plant | To grow vegetables in the desert | That plants are holier than people | That shade from the sun is a reward from God for obedience
exercise: How do Christians differ about the book of Jonah? | Some read it as history, others as a teaching story | Some think Jonah was a king | Some think the fish was a ship | Some think Nineveh was in Egypt
exercise: What did Isaiah say would happen to swords in the peaceable kingdom? | They would be beaten into ploughshares | They would be given to the children to play with | They would be buried deep in the sand of the desert | They would be hung up on the walls of the Temple
test: my cousin: «daniel went in the furnace and jonah got the lions». fix it | Daniel was in the lions' den; his three friends were in the furnace | Jonah was in the furnace and Daniel in the fish | Daniel was in the fish and Jonah in the den | All four of them were thrown to the lions together, and none was hurt
test: «just bow to the stupid statue, nobody's gonna know, ugh come ON». What did the three friends answer? | They would stay faithful even if God did not save them | They would bow down outside but keep praying to God secretly inside | They would ask the king for more time | They would bow once and never again
test: A Muslim classmate fasting in Ramadan says: «it's hard to keep my faith's rules at school». Which story fits best? | Daniel, who kept God's food laws respectfully far from home | Jonah, who ran away from God | Solomon, who turned to other gods | Goliath, who mocked his enemies
test: Your mate: «the school's new rule is no talking at lunch. like daniel, we should all ignore it». What is the best answer? | Daniel refused a law against praying to God, not every rule he disliked | Daniel ignored every rule in Babylon | Daniel would shout louder than anyone | Daniel would leave the school at once
test: «the guy in the whale story was a hero who went straight where god said». right? | No, Jonah first ran away the other way | Yes, Jonah went at once, gladly and quickly | No, Jonah never actually got to Nineveh at all | Yes, and the whale carried him all the way there
test: «nineveh were the baddies so god shoulda smashed them, done». What does the book of Jonah say? | God's mercy is for everyone, even enemies who repent | God always destroys bad cities | Nineveh was never forgiven, however sorry its people were | Only Jonah's people can be saved
test: A friend with no religion asks: «do you seriously think a guy lived in a fish?» A fair answer? | Christians differ: some read it as history, others as a teaching story about second chances | All Christians say it was a submarine | All Christians say it is a joke | The Bible says it was only a dream
test: Your report says «talks in class constantly, will fail if not fixed». What is this like? | The writing on the wall, a clear warning | Isaiah's peaceable kingdom, where the wolf lives with the lamb | The lions' den | The plant that gave shade
test: You were upset when a broken toy was thrown out, but didn't care about the new kid crying. Which story asks you to think again? | Jonah's plant, about caring for people more than things | Daniel's food, about keeping the rules of your faith far from home | The fiery furnace, about not bowing | The writing on the wall, about pride
test: A judge asks: «Who speaks like a prophet today?» | People who speak up for those treated unfairly and tell the truth to power | Anyone who can guess the future | Only kings and presidents | People who are famous online
test: A judge follows up: «Why does the book of Jonah end with a question?» | So every reader must answer whether they care about others as God does | Because the last page was lost | Because Jonah did not know the answer either and simply stopped writing there | So that the story would be shorter
test: «what would swords into ploughs even look like now lol». Best example? | Turning money for weapons into money for farms and schools | Building bigger and bigger armies so that nobody dares to start a war | Making plough-shaped swords | Banning all farming
exam: Identify the conduct for which, according to the Book of Daniel, Daniel was consigned to the den of lions. | His persistence in prayer to God notwithstanding a royal prohibition | His refusal to consume the provisions of the royal table | His theft of gold from the royal treasury | His flight from the city of Babylon
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the response of the three companions of Daniel to the royal command? | They declined to venerate the image, avowing fidelity even should deliverance not be granted | They venerated the image upon receiving assurance of safety | They petitioned the king for exemption on grounds of age | They fled the realm prior to the promulgation of the decree
exam: It is given that the inhabitants of Nineveh repented subsequent to the preaching of Jonah. Determine the disposition of the prophet in respect of their deliverance. | He was displeased that the city was spared | He rejoiced exceedingly at their salvation | He departed immediately for Jerusalem | He remained ignorant of their deliverance
exam: Select the response consistent with the established text concerning the vision of Isaiah. | Swords are to be beaten into ploughshares, and the wolf shall dwell with the lamb | The wolf shall devour the lamb, and swords shall be multiplied across all the earth | Ploughs are to be beaten into swords for a final war | All animals shall be removed from the land
